quectel-CM: IPv6 is optional while IPv4 is up

3GPP TS 23.401 / RFC 6459: a UE requests a dual-stack (IPv4v6) bearer and
accepts what the network assigns; ModemManager treats the connection as up when
either family succeeded. quectel-CM treated a missing IPv6 bearer as a failed
call and retried it on a 5..60 s ladder forever, contending for the QMI channel
and delaying IPv4 recovery. This gives IPv6 its own back-off (60 s doubling to
32 min, reset on a new bearer cycle or a fresh attach); the IPv4 ladder and the
global SetupCallAllowTime are untouched, and IPv6-only configurations behave as
before.

diff --git a/application/quectel_CM_5G_M/src/main.c b/application/quectel_CM_5G_M/src/main.c
index 4cc9345..3146746 100644
--- a/application/quectel_CM_5G_M/src/main.c
+++ b/application/quectel_CM_5G_M/src/main.c
@@ -261,6 +261,19 @@ static int usage(const char *progname) {
     return 0;
 }
 
+/* IPv6 is optional while IPv4 is up (3GPP TS 23.401 / RFC 6459: a UE asks for a
+ * dual-stack bearer and accepts what the network assigns). A failed IPv6 setup
+ * must neither hammer the QMI channel nor disturb the working IPv4 session, so
+ * the IPv6-only retry back-off is separate from the IPv4 ladder:
+ * 60 s, 120 s ... capped at 32 min. It restarts on a new bearer cycle. */
+static unsigned long ipv6_retry_delay_ms(unsigned fail)
+{
+    unsigned shift = (fail > 0) ? (fail - 1) : 0;
+    if (shift > 5)
+        shift = 5;
+    return (60UL << shift) * 1000UL;
+}
